module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Adjoin.FG
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.FiniteType
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Away.AdjoinRoot

/-!
# Trimming an algebra to a finitely generated subalgebra with the same localizations

Let `R` be a commutative ring and `T` an `R`-algebra. Suppose that for finitely many `f i : R`
the localizations `T[1/f i]` are of finite type over `R`. Then **finitely many elements of `T`
already generate an `R`-subalgebra whose localization at each `f i` is all of `T[1/f i]`**:
`exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family`.

This is the algebra analogue of the last paragraph of the proof of [Stacks, Tag 01PF], which
is the step the module-level file
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Affine/Basic.lean` performs for a *span* of sections
(`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.Tag01PFAffine.exists_finite_spanning_subset`): there the submodule
generated by the numerators of local generators is taken, here the subalgebra. It is the ring
level of the "affine cut-down to finite type" that
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/SubalgebraSectionsResidue/Basic.lean` records as one of the
three absent pieces of the symmetric-algebra-free route to the colimit assertion of
[Stacks, Tag 05JT] and to [Stacks, Tag 0817].

## Why the conclusion is phrased with numerators and not with `⊤`

If the `f i` generate the unit ideal of `R` the conclusion upgrades to
`Algebra.adjoin R G = ⊤`, i.e. to finite type of `T` itself — which is already in Mathlib, as
`Algebra.FiniteType.of_span_eq_top_target`. What a cut-down needs is the version in which the
`f i` cover only a *quasi-compact open*, where `T` itself is **not** of finite type and the
subalgebra is genuinely smaller: the useful output is then the subalgebra together with the
statement that it has not shrunk over the open, which is exactly
`HasNumeratorIn (Algebra.adjoin R G) (f i)` holding for every element of `T[1/f i]`.

## Main definitions

* `HasNumeratorIn S f x`: the element `x` of a localization of `T` away from `f` is a fraction
  `t / f ^ n` with numerator `t` in the subalgebra `S`. Membership of the subalgebra generated
  by `G` for every element of `T[1/f]` says precisely that `(Algebra.adjoin R G)[1/f]` is all
  of `T[1/f]`.

## Main results

* `hasNumeratorIn_algebraMap`, `hasNumeratorIn_add`, `hasNumeratorIn_mul`: the fractions with
  numerator in a fixed subalgebra form a subalgebra. These are what let the induction over
  `Algebra.adjoin_induction` go through with no choice of common denominator in sight.
* `exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn`: **one `f`.** If `T[1/f]` is of finite type over `R`, finitely
  many elements of `T` generate a subalgebra over which every element of `T[1/f]` is a
  fraction.
* `exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family`: **finitely many `f i`**, with a single finite subset of
  `T` serving all of them.
* `finiteType_of_isLocalizationAway`: a localization of `R` away from one element is of finite
  type over `R`, so the hypothesis `Algebra.FiniteType R (Tl i)` used above is implied by the
  form a geometric caller holds, namely finite type of `T[1/f i]` over `R[1/f i]`.
* `exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family_of_localizedBase`: the family statement with that
  conversion already performed, i.e. with the hypothesis read over `R[1/f i]`.

## Implementation notes

The hypothesis is finite type over `R`, not over `R[1/f]`, because that is the weaker of the
two and is what the proof uses; `finiteType_of_isLocalizationAway` together with
`Algebra.FiniteType.trans` converts the geometric form into it. No scalar tower between the
`Tl i` is needed or assumed: the statement is a conjunction over `i`, and the only datum shared
between the indices is the finite subset of `T`.

`IsLocalization.surj` is used rather than `IsLocalization.mk'_surjective`, because the former
produces the numerator and the denominator together and `mk'` would have to be unfolded. One
snag: destructing membership of `Submonoid.powers g` yields the exponent equation in
the **unreduced** form `(fun k => g ^ k) n = d`, so `rw` cannot use it — its pattern is the
beta-redex, which does not occur in the goal. Retyping it with a `have` at the reduced form
(`have hn' : algebraMap R T f ^ n = (d : T) := hn`) is accepted by definitional unfolding and
then rewrites. This is the `rw`-matches-at-reducible-transparency shape again, in its cheapest
form.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01PF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01PF)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05JT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05JT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0817](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0817)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe v w

namespace Stacks.SubalgebraTrimLocalization

variable {R T : Type*} [CommRing R] [CommRing T] [Algebra R T]

section OneElement

variable {Tl : Type*} [CommRing Tl] [Algebra T Tl] [Algebra R Tl] [IsScalarTower R T Tl]

/-- **An element of a localization of `T` away from `f` which is a fraction with numerator in
the subalgebra `S`.** Over a basic open `D(f)` of an affine scheme the sections of a
quasi-coherent algebra sheaf are such a localization, and this predicate holding for every
element says that the subsheaf cut out by `S` has not shrunk over `D(f)`. -/
def HasNumeratorIn (S : Subalgebra R T) (f : R) (x : Tl) : Prop :=
  ∃ (t : T) (n : ℕ), t ∈ S ∧ x * algebraMap R Tl f ^ n = algebraMap T Tl t

variable (S : Subalgebra R T) (f : R)

/-- The denominator `f`, viewed in the localization, is the image of its own image in `T`. -/
theorem algebraMap_algebraMap :
    algebraMap T Tl (algebraMap R T f) = algebraMap R Tl f :=
  (IsScalarTower.algebraMap_apply R T Tl f).symm

/-- The image of a scalar is a fraction with denominator `1`. -/
theorem hasNumeratorIn_algebraMap (r : R) :
    HasNumeratorIn (Tl := Tl) S f (algebraMap R Tl r) := by
  refine ⟨algebraMap R T r, 0, S.algebraMap_mem r, ?_⟩
  rw [pow_zero, mul_one, algebraMap_algebraMap]

variable {S f}

/-- Fractions with numerator in `S` are closed under addition: clear denominators, which is
legitimate because `S` contains the image of `f`. -/
theorem hasNumeratorIn_add {x y : Tl} (hx : HasNumeratorIn S f x)
    (hy : HasNumeratorIn S f y) : HasNumeratorIn S f (x + y) := by
  obtain ⟨t, n, htS, ht⟩ := hx
  obtain ⟨s, m, hsS, hs⟩ := hy
  refine ⟨t * algebraMap R T f ^ m + s * algebraMap R T f ^ n, n + m,
    add_mem (mul_mem htS (pow_mem (S.algebraMap_mem f) m))
      (mul_mem hsS (pow_mem (S.algebraMap_mem f) n)), ?_⟩
  rw [map_add, map_mul, map_mul, map_pow, map_pow, algebraMap_algebraMap (Tl := Tl),
    ← ht, ← hs, pow_add]
  ring

omit [IsScalarTower R T Tl] in
/-- Fractions with numerator in `S` are closed under multiplication, with no clearing of
denominators needed. -/
theorem hasNumeratorIn_mul {x y : Tl} (hx : HasNumeratorIn S f x)
    (hy : HasNumeratorIn S f y) : HasNumeratorIn S f (x * y) := by
  obtain ⟨t, n, htS, ht⟩ := hx
  obtain ⟨s, m, hsS, hs⟩ := hy
  refine ⟨t * s, n + m, mul_mem htS hsS, ?_⟩
  rw [map_mul, ← ht, ← hs, pow_add]
  ring

omit [IsScalarTower R T Tl] in
/-- Enlarging the subalgebra preserves the property. -/
theorem HasNumeratorIn.mono {S S' : Subalgebra R T} (h : S ≤ S') {f : R} {x : Tl}
    (hx : HasNumeratorIn S f x) : HasNumeratorIn S' f x := by
  obtain ⟨t, n, htS, ht⟩ := hx
  exact ⟨t, n, h htS, ht⟩

/-- **Every element of a localization of `T` away from `f` is a fraction, and finitely many
numerators suffice.** If `T[1/f]` is of finite type over `R`, there is a finite subset `G` of
`T` such that every element of `T[1/f]` is a fraction with numerator in the `R`-subalgebra
generated by `G`; equivalently, the localization of that subalgebra at `f` is already all of
`T[1/f]`.

The numerators are those of a finite generating set of `T[1/f]` over `R`: the fractions with
numerator in the subalgebra they generate form a subalgebra containing the generators, hence
everything. -/
theorem exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn (f : R) [IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R T f) Tl]
    [Algebra.FiniteType R Tl] :
    ∃ G : Finset T, ∀ x : Tl, HasNumeratorIn (Algebra.adjoin R (G : Set T)) f x := by
  classical
  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := (Algebra.FiniteType.out : (⊤ : Subalgebra R Tl).FG)
  have hnum : ∀ y : Tl, ∃ p : T × ℕ,
      y * algebraMap R Tl f ^ p.2 = algebraMap T Tl p.1 := by
    intro y
    obtain ⟨⟨t, d⟩, hd⟩ :=
      IsLocalization.surj (M := Submonoid.powers (algebraMap R T f)) (S := Tl) y
    obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := d.2
    have hn' : algebraMap R T f ^ n = (d : T) := hn
    refine ⟨⟨t, n⟩, ?_⟩
    rw [← algebraMap_algebraMap (R := R) (T := T) (Tl := Tl) f, ← map_pow, hn']
    exact hd
  choose num hnum using hnum
  refine ⟨s.image fun y => (num y).1, fun x => ?_⟩
  have hx : x ∈ Algebra.adjoin R (s : Set Tl) := by rw [hs]; exact Algebra.mem_top
  induction hx using Algebra.adjoin_induction with
  | mem y hy =>
      refine ⟨(num y).1, (num y).2, Algebra.subset_adjoin ?_, hnum y⟩
      exact Finset.mem_coe.mpr (Finset.mem_image_of_mem _ (Finset.mem_coe.mp hy))
  | algebraMap r => exact hasNumeratorIn_algebraMap _ f r
  | add _ _ _ _ hx hy => exact hasNumeratorIn_add hx hy
  | mul _ _ _ _ hx hy => exact hasNumeratorIn_mul hx hy

end OneElement

/-- **A localization of `R` away from one element is of finite type over `R`.** This is what
turns the geometric hypothesis — the sections of the algebra over `D(f)` are of finite type over
the sections of the structure sheaf over `D(f)` — into the hypothesis
`Algebra.FiniteType R (Tl i)` of `exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family`, by
`Algebra.FiniteType.trans`. -/
theorem finiteType_of_isLocalizationAway {Rl : Type*} [CommRing Rl] [Algebra R Rl] (f : R)
    [IsLocalization.Away f Rl] : Algebra.FiniteType R Rl :=
  have : Algebra.FinitePresentation R Rl :=
    Algebra.FinitePresentation.of_isLocalizationAway (R := R) (S := R) (S' := Rl) f
  Algebra.FiniteType.of_finitePresentation

/-- **Finitely many denominators, one finite set of numerators.** Let `f i : R` be finitely many
elements and let `Tl i` be a localization of the `R`-algebra `T` away from `f i`, of finite type
over `R`. Then a single finite subset `G` of `T` works for every index: every element of every
`Tl i` is a fraction with numerator in the `R`-subalgebra generated by `G`.

This is the ring-level form of the affine cut-down of [Stacks, Tag 01PF] for algebras: the `f i`
are the basic opens covering the quasi-compact open over which the subalgebra must not shrink,
and `Algebra.adjoin R G` is the finitely generated subalgebra the cut-down produces. -/
theorem exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family {ι : Type w} [Finite ι] (f : ι → R)
    (Tl : ι → Type v) [∀ i, CommRing (Tl i)] [∀ i, Algebra T (Tl i)] [∀ i, Algebra R (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, IsScalarTower R T (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R T (f i)) (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, Algebra.FiniteType R (Tl i)] :
    ∃ G : Finset T, ∀ (i : ι) (x : Tl i),
      HasNumeratorIn (Algebra.adjoin R (G : Set T)) (f i) x := by
  classical
  cases nonempty_fintype ι
  have h : ∀ i : ι, ∃ G : Finset T,
      ∀ x : Tl i, HasNumeratorIn (Algebra.adjoin R (G : Set T)) (f i) x :=
    fun i => exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn (T := T) (Tl := Tl i) (f i)
  choose G hG using h
  refine ⟨Finset.univ.biUnion G, fun i x => (hG i x).mono (Algebra.adjoin_mono ?_)⟩
  intro a ha
  exact Finset.mem_coe.mpr
    (Finset.mem_biUnion.mpr ⟨i, Finset.mem_univ i, Finset.mem_coe.mp ha⟩)

/-- **The same conclusion from finite type over the localized base**, which is the form a
geometric caller holds: on an affine scheme `Spec R` with a quasi-coherent algebra sheaf `A`, the
sections over a basic open `D(f i)` are of finite type over `Γ(X, D(f i)) = R[1/f i]`, not over
`R`. The two hypotheses are reconciled by `finiteType_of_isLocalizationAway` and
`Algebra.FiniteType.trans`, so a caller need not perform that step. -/
theorem exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family_of_localizedBase {ι : Type w} [Finite ι] (f : ι → R)
    (Rl : ι → Type v) (Tl : ι → Type v) [∀ i, CommRing (Rl i)] [∀ i, CommRing (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, Algebra R (Rl i)] [∀ i, IsLocalization.Away (f i) (Rl i)]
    [∀ i, Algebra T (Tl i)] [∀ i, Algebra R (Tl i)] [∀ i, IsScalarTower R T (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, IsLocalization.Away (algebraMap R T (f i)) (Tl i)]
    [∀ i, Algebra (Rl i) (Tl i)] [∀ i, IsScalarTower R (Rl i) (Tl i)]
    (hft : ∀ i, Algebra.FiniteType (Rl i) (Tl i)) :
    ∃ G : Finset T, ∀ (i : ι) (x : Tl i),
      HasNumeratorIn (Algebra.adjoin R (G : Set T)) (f i) x :=
  have : ∀ i, Algebra.FiniteType R (Tl i) := fun i =>
    (finiteType_of_isLocalizationAway (R := R) (Rl := Rl i) (f i)).trans (hft i)
  exists_finset_hasNumeratorIn_family f Tl

end Stacks.SubalgebraTrimLocalization

end
